## 1. Introduction
The extensive use of pesticides enables the effective control of pests (weeds, insects or pathogens), thus performing an important function in sustainable agricultural development and good public health. Less than $0.1\%$ of the applied pesticides, however, can effectively act against the target organisms. Moreover, most are lost in the environment because of irrational pesticide use and underdeveloped pesticide formulation, causing environmental pollution and non-target risks [1]. In view of this, it has become an urgent demand to improve the utilization efficiency of pesticides. Fortunately, controlled release formulations (CRFs) afford a new dimension for resolving the current problem that confronts the use of pesticides [2–5]. Compared with traditional formulations, CRFs allow the use of minimal amounts of pesticides to effectively improve the utilization efficiency of pesticides and reduce environmental pollution [6]. To achieve this worthwhile objective, the elaborate selection of carrier materials and formulation techniques is critical.
Over the previous years, structured porous materials are widely applied as drug delivery vehicles because of their remarkable features, such as facile synthesis, tunable morphology and pore size, large pore volume and surface area, and stability in a biological system [7,8]. These exceptional characteristics can encapsulate drugs with a high loading capacity and release the cargo molecules in a sustained and controlled manners [9,10]. Recently, we have developed several CRFs of pesticides using inorganic porous materials as scaffolds, particularly mesoporous silica nanoparticles (MSNs). The stimuli-responsive release profiles as well as the translocation, distribution, and degradation behaviors of pesticide-loaded MSNs are also well investigated [11–14]. In the continuous screening of porous materials as pesticide carriers, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), as an emerging class of hybrid inorganic–organic porous materials, have been the focus of interest. These frameworks, which are produced from metal ions or clusters linked by organic ligands, exhibit distinctive properties, such as exceptionally high surface areas, excellent functionalities, and adjustable pores [15–18]. Metal–organic frameworks have been extensively investigated and applied in gas storage and separation [18,19], water harvesting [20,21], heterogeneous catalysis [22,23], chemical sensors [24,25], environmental remediation [26,27], drug delivery [28,29], and others.
In view of their excellent performance, MOFs have a considerable application potential in the field of sustainable agriculture field. Most of the developments in agriculture, however, focused on food safety [30], and pesticide sensing and removal [31,32]; the studies on MOF-based platforms for plant protection and nutrition have just started. Yaghi et al. developed eco-friendly MOFs constructed from ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ ions and naturally occurring renewable lactate linkers. These construction materials can encapsulate an agriculturally important fumigant, cis-1,3-dichloropropene, which exhibits a release rate that is 100 times lower than that of liquid cis-1,3-dichloropropene [33]. Zhang et al. prepared ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ -based MOFs used for the encapsulation of plant hormone ethylene to manage the ripening of climacteric banana and avocado fruits [34]. Porphyrinic MOFs built from Zr metal ions clusters (IV) as nodes and meso-tetra(4-carboxyphenyl)porphine as an organic linker were applied to load and control the release of tebuconazole pesticide [35]. Recently, $\upbeta$ -cyclodextrin-based MOFs have been fabricated and simultaneously used as herbicide removal agent and potassium fertilizer source [36].
Among the plethora of MOFs, iron-based MOFs (Fe-MOFs) have an excellent potential for practical applications in many fields. Compared with other metals, the iron offers several advantages, including its nontoxicity, low cost, preferable stability, and environmental compatibility [37]. Moreover, iron is an essential micronutrient for crop growth and performs an important function in the photosynthetic reactions [38]. Iron deficiency extremely alters the plant growth and metabolism, such as chlorophyll synthesis, nitrogen fixation, and respiration [39]. Accordingly, various existing forms of iron fertilizers, including inorganic iron [39,40], chelating iron [40,41], slow-releasing iron [42], and iron nanoparticles [43,44] have been explored to test their nutritional quality for different crops. It is noteworthy that Fe-MOFs have also been applied as an iron fertilizer to promote the plant growth rate [45,46]. It is thus evident that Fe-MOFs have a considerable prospect in agriculture as multifunctional materials for both pesticide delivery and plant nutrient replenishment.
22As a type of important broad-spectrum agricultural fungicide, azoxystrobin (AZOX, Fig. 1) can effectively prevent and/or cure foliar disease caused by a major pathogenic plant fungus [47]. Azoxystrobin is thus selected as a model pesticide to test the adsorption capacity and controlled release performance of Fe-MOFs. In this work, a simple Fe-MOFs built from trimers of iron octahedra linked by 1, 3, 5-benzenetricarboxylate are used as carriers for the controlled release of AZOX. This facile-prepared Fe-MOFs are conventionally named Fe-MIL-100 (MIL: Materials of Institut Lavoisier), which have been previously used for drug delivery [28,48]. In the present study, the controlled and sustained release behaviors of AZOX from pesticide-loaded Fe-MIL-100 as well as its fungicidal bioactivities against wheat head scab (Fusarium graminearum) and tomato late blight (Phytophthora infestans) were investigated. The visual observation of Fe-MIL-100 in the mycelium was also conducted. Furthermore, the nutritional function of Fe-MIL-100 as iron fertilizer for enhancing wheat growth was demonstrated.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
Among the materials, 1, 3, 5-Benzenetricarboxylic acid (1, 3, 5- BTC, $98\%$ ) was obtained from Alfa Aesar (Shanghai, China). Iron powder $(98\%)$ and nitric acid $(65\%)$ were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. (Beijing, China). Hydrofluoric acid (HF, $35\mathrm{wt\%}$ solution in ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ ), fluorescein isothiocyanate (FITC, $95\%$ , and ethylenediamine (EDA, $98\%$ ) were purchased from J&K Scientific Ltd. (Beijing, China). Azoxystrobin suspension concentrates (AZOX SC, $25\%$ was purchased from Beijing Green Agricultural Science and Technology Group Co., Ltd. The solvent of AZOX SC is water, and other constituents are pesticide adjuvants. Azoxystrobin technical concentrate (AZOX TC, $98\%$ ) was supplied by Jiangsu Anpon Electrochemical Co., Ltd. The Pesticide Bioassay Lab in Institute of Plant Protection, Chinese Academy of Agricultural Sciences generously provided the tomato late blight (P. infestans) and wheat head scab (F. graminearum). All other chemicals were commercially available and used as received.
## 2.2. Syntheses of Fe-MIL-100 and FITC-labeled Fe-MIL-100
Iron octahedra linked by 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylate (Fe-MIL100) was synthesized by employing a slightly modified version of the hydrothermal method reported by Huo et al. [49]. In this technique, $277.5~\mathrm{mg}$ of iron powder, $687.5~\mathrm{mg}$ of 1, 3, 5-BTC, $200~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of HF $(35\%)$ , $190\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of nitric acid $(65\%)$ were well dispersed by ultrasound in a $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of ultrapure water. The mixture was thereafter loaded into a Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave, which was placed in an oven maintained at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The lightorange solid that forms during the process was collected by filtration and washed several times with ultrapure water. To remove the residual unreacted ions, the as-prepared light-orange solid was further purified with boiling water at for $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ , followed by hot ethanol for $3\mathrm{h}$ . The highly purified product was thereafter separated by centrifugation at $10\ 000\ \mathrm{rpm}$ for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ . Finally, the solid was vacuum-dried overnight at $80^{\circ}C$ .
Fluorescein isothiocyanate-labelled Fe-MIL-100 (FITC-Fe-MIL100) was prepared using a two-steps procedure. Firstly, the aminefunctionalizedFe-MIL-100 ( $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -Fe-MIL-100)wassynthesizedusing a modified version of the procedure reported by Hwang et al. [50]. In the aforementioned technique, $50\mathrm{mg}$ of Fe-MIL-100 was suspended in $50\mathrm{mL}$ of anhydrous ethanol. Thereafter, $0.5\mathrm{mL}$ of EDA was added, and the mixture was stirred under reflux for 6 h. The precipitate was then collected by centrifugation at $10000\ \mathrm{rpm}$ for $5~\mathrm{min}$ , washed three times with ethanol, and dried at room temperature. When $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -Fe-MIL-100 was obtained, FITC-Fe-MIL-100 was subsequently synthesized as follows. Five milligrams of FITC and $200\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ - Fe-MIL-100 were dissolved and suspended in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of anhydrous ethanol. The reaction mixture was stirred at room temperature and shielded from light for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The resulting products were washed with ethanol thrice and dried at $50^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ overnight.
## 2.3. Sample characterizations
The structural and morphological features of as-prepared particles were analyzed by scanning electron microscopy (SEM, SU8000, Hitachi, Ltd., Tokyo, Japan) with a working distance of $14.9\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and beam energy of $30.0\ \mathrm{kV}$ . The Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra were recorded on a spectrometer (NICOLET 6700, Thermo Scientific, Waltham, MA, USA) in the spectral region of 400 to $4000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The thermal properties of materials were investigated by thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA) using a PerkinElmer Pyris Diamond (Woodland, CA) with a nitrogen flow of $20~\mathrm{mL/min}$ for the balance and $30\ \mathrm{mL/min}$ for the sample. The measurements were performed in a stream of $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ with a heating rate of $10\ \mathrm{^{\circ}C/m i n}$ from $20^{\circ}\mathsf C$ to $550^{\circ}\mathsf C$ .
To analyze the elemental composition of samples, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was performed using a photoelectron spectrometer (ESCALab 250Xi, Thermo Fisher Scientific) with a $150~\mathrm{W}$ monochromatic Al $\mathsf{K}\mathsf{\pmb{\upalpha}}$ radiation ( $1486.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ ; $500~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ spot size) as the excitation source. The binding energies were calibrated by the C 1 s peak of the surface carbon at 284.8 eV. To characterize the elemental composition of crystals, energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) mapping was employed.
The X-ray powder diffraction (XRD) patterns of the samples were recorded on a Bruker D8 advance X-ray diffractometer $\mathrm{CuK}\mathfrak{a}$ radiation, $\lambda=0.15418\mathrm{nm}$ ) equipped with a LynxEye array detector at a scanning rate of $0.1^{\circ}/s$ in a 2 theta range from $10^{\circ}$ to $90^{\circ}$ . The operating voltage and current were $40\mathrm{kV}$ and $40\mathrm{mA}$ , respectively.
The specific surface area and pore size distribution were examined by nitrogen adsorption/desorption measurements performed at $-196^{\circ}C$ on a specific surface area and pore size analyzer (TriStarII 3020, Micromeritics Instruments Corp, Norcross, GA). The sample powder was degassed for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ at $120^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and $10^{-3}$ Torr before the adsorption experiment was performed. The specific surface area was calculated by the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) equation at a relative pressure $\left(\mathrm{P/P_{0}}\right)$ of 0.06–0.22 and the pore size distribution was estimated from the adsorption data by the Barrett-JoynerHalenda (BJH) model.
## 2.4. Preparation of AZOX-loaded Fe-MIL-100 (AZOX@Fe-MIL-100)
Approximately ${900}~\mathrm{{mg}}$ of AZOX were dissolved into $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of dichloromethane to obtain an AZOX solution with a $30\mathrm{mg/mL}$ concentration. Fe-MIL-100 with different carrier/pesticide ratios was subsequently dispersed in the aforementioned solution. The mixture was sealed and magnetically stirred for $20\mathrm{h}$ at room temperature; thereafter, AZOX@Fe-MIL-100 were obtained by filtration. Finally, the derived AZOX@Fe-MIL-100 were dried overnight at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ to remove the solvent.
To determine the loading content and encapsulation efficiency of AZOX, approximately $5~\mathrm{mg}$ of the prepared AZOX@Fe-MIL-100 above was dispersed in $5~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol. After ultrasonic treatment for $^\mathrm{~1~h~}$ , the supernatant was collected by centrifugation. The foregoing procedure was repeated four times. The concentration of the combined supernatant was thereafter determined by high-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC, 1200-DAD (Diode Array Detector), Agilent, CA, USA); this determination process was repeated thrice. The loading content and encapsulation efficiency were calculated as follows: loading content $\left(\%\right)=$ (weight of AZOX in AZOX@Fe-MIL-100/weight of $A Z0\times@\mathrm{Fe}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{-}100)~\times~100\%$ ; encapsulation efficiency $\left(\%\right)=$ (weight of AZOX in AZOX $@$ Fe-MIL100/initial weight of AZOX employed) $\times100\%$ .
The operating conditions for HPLC determination were as follows: ZORBAX $S\mathrm{B-C}_{18}$ reversed-phase column $5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ , $4.6\times150\mathrm{m}$ m); column temperature, $25~^{\circ}C$ ; mobile phase, (acetonitrile $/0.1\%$ formic acid aqueous solution $(\mathsf{V}/\mathsf{V})=48{:}52\$ ; flow rate, $1.0~\mathrm{mL}/$ min; the injection volume, $5~{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ ; and DAD signals, $254~\mathrm{{nm}}$ .
## 2.5. In vitro release of AZOX
In order to study the pesticides release behavior of the prepared AZOX@Fe-MIL-100, three release media with different pH values (5.0, 7.2 and 8.5) containing phosphate buffered saline (PBS), ethanol and Tween-80 emulsifier $(70;29.5;0.5,{\mathrm{v}}/{\mathrm{v}}/{\mathrm{v}})$ was prepared. Approximately $20\ \mathrm{\mg}$ of AZOX@Fe-MIL-100 was dispersed in $2.0~\mathrm{mL}$ of release medium in dialysis bags (molecular weight cutoff: 8000–14000 Da). The sealed dialysis bag was thereafter placed in $200~\mathrm{{mL}}$ of release medium at $30^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and stirred at $100\mathrm{rpm}$ using a D-800LS dissolution tester (Tianjin University, China). At a certain time interval, $1.0~\mathrm{mL}$ of the release medium was withdrawn for HPLC analysis, and the same volume of fresh release medium was added. The determination process was performed in triplicates. The accumulative AZOX released was calculated according to the following formulation:
$$
E_{\mathrm{r}}={\frac{V_{\mathrm{e}}\sum_{i=0}^{n\mathrm{~-~}1}C_{i}+V_{0}C_{\mathrm{n}}}{m_{p e s t i c i d e}}}\times100\%
$$
where $E_{r}$ is the accumulative AZOX released $(\%)$ from the $\mathsf{A Z O X@}$ FeMIL-100; $V_{e}=1.0~\mathrm{mL}$ ; $C_{n}$ is the AZOX concentration $\mathrm{(mg/mL)}$ in the release medium at sampling time (n); $V_{0}$ is the volume of release solution $(200~\mathrm{{mL}}$ ); mpesticide is the total quantity of pesticide loaded into Fe-MIL-100 (mg).
## 2.6. Bioactivity studies of AZOX@Fe-MIL-100
To study the fungicidal activity of the prepared AZOX@Fe-MIL100, two different types of fungi, wheat head scab (F. graminearum) and tomato late blight (P. infestans), were used with the growth rate method. The mycelium discs with a diameter of $5~\mathrm{mm}$ was inoculated on potato dextrose agar (PDA) plates. Before the PDA plates was prepared, the sterile molten PDA was treated with a stock solution of AZOX@Fe-MIL-100 to achieve the desired concentrations. As a control, an AZOX technical concentration (AZOX TC) and a $25\%$ commercial formulation of AZOX suspension concentrates (AZOX SC) were also employed to treat the PDA in the bioactivity test. In order to better exert the antibacterial effect of AZOX, $100~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ of salicylic hydroxamic acid (SH) as alternative oxidase inhibitor was utilized in all treatments [51]. After culturing the mycelia at $25\pm2^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for 7–8 d according to the growth rate of the untreated mycelia, the mycelial radial growth was measured. The experiment was repeated five times. The percentage of growth inhibition $(\%)$ expresses the fungicidal activity and was calculated as follows:
Inhibition $(\%)$ colony diameter of control — colony diameter of treatment colony diameter of control — diameter of mycelial discs $\times100\%$
## 2.7. Uptake of Fe-MIL-100 by fungi
To study the uptake of Fe-MOFs by fungi, Fe-MIL-100 was covalently bound to FITC through the introduction of amine group. Accordingly, FITC-Fe-MIL-100 was prepared and used for visual observation. The PDA plates containing $100~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ of FITC-Fe-MIL100 were inoculated with $P.$ . infestans. After a 5 d culture, the mycelia found at the edge of the colony was cautiously collected for imaging observation by confocal laser scanning microscopy (CLSM, Zeiss LSM 880) with a laser excitation wavelength of $488~\mathrm{nm}$ .
## 2.8. Nutritional function of Fe-MIL-100 for wheat growth
Plump wheat seeds of dwarf resistance 58 were used in the experiment. The seeds were surface-sterilized with $0.3\%\mathrm{~(v/v)~}$ sodium hypochlorite for $2~\mathrm{min}$ , rinsed thoroughly with deionized water three times and dried in a sterile oven. Ten similarly sized wheat seeds were placed into a $5~\mathrm{mL}$ centrifuge tube containing $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of Fe-MIL-100 aqueous solution to accelerate germination. The particles of Fe-MIL-100 can be dispersed in water with the assistance of ultrasound. Two different concentrations of 50 and $300~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ were set for Fe-MIL-100 aqueous solution. Deionized water was employed as a blank control. Each treatment was replicated five times. After $24\mathrm{h}$ , the wheat seeds and Fe-MIL-100 aqueous solution in each centrifuge tube were transferred into plastic pots ( $10\mathrm{cm}$ high with diameter of $9.0\mathrm{cm}$ filled with $150{\mathrm{g}}$ of soil. The main constituents of soil are turf, roseite, and perlite. The wheat was grown in a greenhouse over a $24\mathrm{~h~}$ photoperiod with a light intensity of $40~\upmu\mathrm{w}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ . The temperature was maintained at $25\pm2^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , and the humidity was held at $45\text{\textperthousand}$ . Each pot was watered daily with $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of distilled water. One week after sowing, the plant height of the aerial part of the wheat were measured.
## 2.9. Statistical analysis
For the statistical analysis, SPSS 10.0 software (SPSS Inc., Chicago, IL, USA) was employed. One-way analysis of variance followed by Duncan’s multiple range test were used. The mean ± standard deviation (SD) values in each group were determined in triplicate. The P values that are less than or equal to $5\%$ are considered as significant.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Sample preparation and characterization
Fe-MIL-100 was synthesized by a hydrothermal reaction using $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ as the node and 1,3,5-BTC as the organic ligand [49]. The SEM images of Fe-MIL-100 and AZOX@Fe-MIL-100 shown in
The FT-IR spectra of Fe-MIL-100 and AZOX@Fe-MIL-100 are shown in Fig. 3a. The Fe-MIL-100 peaks 1633, 1537, 1453, and $1381~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ of Fe-MIL-100 are attributed to the characteristic absorption of benzene ring. On the other hand, AZOX exhibits characteristic absorptions at 1561 and $2231~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which can be observed in AZOX@Fe-MIL-100, indicating the successful loading of AZOX into Fe-MIL-100 (Fig. 3a and b).
The TGA is always used to explore the thermal stability and decomposition behaviors of materials. The TGA curves of AZOX, Fe-MIL-100 and AZOX@Fe-MIL-100 are depicted in Fig. 4a. The material weight loss that occurs before the temperature reaches $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ may have been caused by the volatilization of water molecules in the sample. The occurrence of a more considerable weight loss when the temperature is between 150 and $500^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ may be the result of the decomposition of organic ligands in the Fe-MIL-100 structure. More weight is lost by AZOX@Fe-MIL-100 (the black curve) than the Fe-MIL-100 (the red curve), indicating the successful loading of AZOX. The total weight losses of Fe-MIL-100 and AZOX@Fe-MIL-100 in the range $20{-}550~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ are approximately $50\%$ and $73\%$ , respectively. Differential thermogravimetric (DTG) curves are always employed to investigate the effect of thermal stabilization on guest molecules loaded into carrier materials [52,53]. The DTG curves of AZOX, Fe-MIL-100, and AZOX@FeMIL-100 in the range $200{-}400~^{\circ}C$ are shown in Fig. 4b. The initial temperature $\left(\mathrm{T_{id}}\right)$ of the material degradation can be identified by the point at which the DTG curves start to deviate from their baselines [52]. Although it is difficult to obtain the accurate $\mathrm{T_{id}}$ value of AZOX from AZOX@Fe-MIL-100 because of the overlap with Fe-MIL-100, the shift in the peak value towards a higher AZOX@FeMIL-100 temperature clearly indicates the effect of thermal stabilization on AZOX loaded into Fe-MIL-100.
The elements information on the surface of the materials can be obtained from the XPS spectra. The XPS spectra of Fe-MIL100 and AZOX@Fe-MIL-100 are presented in Fig. 5a. In the FeMIL-100 spectrum, the binding energies of approximately 284.8 eV, 531.7 eV, and $711.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ belong to C1s, O1s, and Fe2p, respectively. As shown in Fig. 5b, the EDS mapping spectrum of Fe-MIL-100 also clearly reveals the presence of carbon, oxygen, and iron elements. In the AZOX@Fe-MIL-100 spectrum, the newly emerging signal at $399.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ is attributed to N1s (Fig. 5a). The EDS mapping spectrum of AZOX@Fe-MIL-100 (Fig. 5c) that corresponds to the XPS spectrum also clearly reveals the presence of nitrogen element, further confirming that AZOX is successfully loaded into Fe-MIL-100.
The porous structures of Fe-MIL-100 and AZOX@Fe-MIL-100 were examined by the BET specific surface area and BJH pore size and volume analyses. Fig. 6 shows the $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms and pore size distributions of Fe-MIL-100 and AZOX@Fe-MIL-100. A type I isotherm is exhibited by Fe-MIL-100, and two secondary uptakes of 0.06 and 0.12 are at ${\sf P}/{\sf P}_{0}$ , revealing that the sample has two types of microporous structures [45]. Table 1 summarizes the values of the BET specific surface area $(S_{\mathrm{BET}})$ , the total pore volume $(\mathsf{V}_{\mathrm{t}})$ , and the BJH pore diameter $(\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{BJH}})$ of Fe-MIL-100 and AZOX@Fe-MIL-100. After loading of AZOX, the pores of Fe-MIL-100 are filled, and the $S_{\mathrm{BET}}$ and $\mathsf{V}_{\mathrm{t}}$ are reduced from 2251 to $1199\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and from 0.89 to $0.46~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ , respectively, suggesting that most the micropores are occupied by the AZOX molecules. To elucidate the contribution of loaded AZOX molecules in the pores on the surface area and pore volume, $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption experiments for AZOX@Fe-MIL-100 with lower loading content of $7.2\%$ were performed. As shown in Table 1, with the increasing of loading content of AZOX, the $S_{\mathrm{BET}}$ and $\mathsf{V}_{\mathrm{t}}$ of AZOX $@$ Fe-MIL-100 decrease obviously. These results clearly indicate that the occupation of micropores with guest molecules would affect the gas adsorption/desorption properties of MOFs. Moreover, the XRD patterns of Fe-MIL-100 before and after loading of AZOX molecules were determined. As evidenced by XRD (Fig. 7), the crystalline structure of Fe-MIL-100 is basically kept after loading of AZOX molecules.
## 3.2. Pesticide loading
The loading content and encapsulation efficiency of AZOX were optimized by adjusting the pesticide–carrier ratio; the results under various conditions are summarized in Table 2. As expected, the loading content increases with the increasing of AZOX, possibly because of the higher AZOX concentration, which promotes the carrier’s adsorption of pesticide molecules. The adsorption of the Fe-MIL-100 on pesticide molecules reaches up to $16.2\%$ when the pesticide–carrier ratio is 3:1 (entry 4, Table 2). When the ratio is increased, however, the encapsulation efficiency gradually decreases. By simultaneously considering the loading content and encapsulation efficiency together, the large-scale of AZOX@FeMIL-100 samples were prepared with a pesticide–carrier ratio of 3:1 for sample characterization, controlled release and bioactivity assays.
## 3.3. Controlled release of AZOX
Fig. 8a shows the release behaviors of the AZOX@Fe-MIL-100 at three different pH values, i.e., 5, 7.2, and 8.5. In view of the low water solubility of AZOX, the mixture solution of PBS, ethanol, and Tween-80 emulsifier $(70;~29.5;~0.5,~\mathrm{v/v/v})$ was adopted as the release medium. All the AZOX-loaded Fe-MOFs indicated slow and sustained release. The release, however, is pH sensitive. At pH 5.0, the release is slower than those under neutral and alkaline conditions. Compared to the release under acidic conditions, AZOX@Fe-MIL-100 exhibits a faster release rate in the first $500~\mathrm{{min}}$ under neutral and alkaline conditions. The accumulative release of AZOX only reaches $63\%$ under the acidic condition after $1500\ \mathrm{min}$ , whereas at $\mathsf{p H}7.2$ and 8.5, the corresponding release values reached approximately $80\%$ . In acidic solution, carboxylic groups (–COOH) of Fe-MIL-100 can form hydrogen bonding with AZOX to retard the release of AZOX from AZOX@Fe-MIL-100. While in neutral or alkaline solution, the corresponding hydrogen bonding interactions disappear due to the completely deprotonation of carboxylic groups in Fe-MIL-100 facilitating the release of cargo molecules. After $2500\ \mathrm{min}$ , the release of AZOX under different conditions reached plateaus with the accumulative release values of approximately $80\%$ $\mathrm{\cdotpH}5.0\mathrm{\cdot}$ , $85\%$ (pH 7.2), and $86\%$ $\operatorname{\cdotpH}8.5\operatorname{\cdot}$ .
To further elucidate the release mechanism, the data of AZOX release data from AZOX@Fe-MIL-100 at pH values of 5.0, 7.2, and 8.5 were fitted using the Korsmeyer-Pappas equation [54]; the fitting plots are shown in Fig. 8b. The kinetic parameters obtained from this fitting are summarized in Table 3, where $k$ and $n$ are the kinetics constant and the release exponent, respectively. The n value indicates the type of release mechanism. It is observed that n values are lower than 0.45 when pH values are 7.2 and 8.5 for AZOX release, indicating a quasi-Fickian diffusion mechanism. On the other hand, when pH is 5.0, n exceeds 0.45 but lower than 0.89, indicating a non-Fickian transport. The release results demonstrate that pH value not only affects the release rate but also influences the release mechanism.
## 3.4. Bioactivity
The fungicidal activity of AZOX@Fe-MIL-100 was determined by the mycelium growth rate method. The bioactivities of blank carriers of Fe-MIL-100, AZOX TC, and AZOX SC are also tested as controls. In order to better exert the fungicidal activity of AZOX, $100~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ of SH as alternative oxidase inhibitor was used in all treatments. SH are frequently used with strobilurin fungicides [55–59]. The inhibition percentages of different AZOX formulations, blank carrier of Fe-MIL-100, $100~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ of $\mathrm{\sfSH}$ , and Fe-MIL100 with $100~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ of SH on wheat head scab (F. graminearum) and tomato late blight (P. infestans) are shown in Fig. 9, and the images of colonies are shown in Fig. 10. The concentrations of the active ingredient of AZOX were set to 5 and $15\mathrm{mg/L}$ . As a result of the presence of SH, the $100\mathrm{mg/L}$ SH and blank carrier of Fe-MIL100 with $100\mathrm{mg/L}$ SH have noticeable bioactivities against the two fungi with approximately $20\%$ inhibition. The blank carrier of FeMIL-100 with no SH, however, exhibits no fungicidal bioactivity against the two fungi.
For the control of P. infestans, the inhibitions of AZOX@Fe-MIL100 at concentrations of 5 and $15~\mathrm{mg/L}$ are found to be $56\%$ and $62\%$ on the 7th day, respectively; the corresponding inhibitions of AZOX TC and AZOX SC are $64\%$ and $69\%$ ) and $64\%$ and $67\%$ ), respectively. On the 4th day, the inhibitions of AZOX@Fe-MIL100 against F. graminearum are $66\%$ and $68\%$ , whereas those of AZOX TC and AZOX SC are ( $68\%$ and $75\%$ ) and ( $68\%$ and $75\%$ ), respectively. The findings clearly indicate that AZOX@Fe-MIL-100 has a fungicidal bioactivity against the two types of fungi that is comparable to those of AZOX TC and SC.
## 3.5. Uptake of FITC-labelled Fe-MIL-100 in fungi
The uptake and translocation of carriers in the biological system is advantageous for the bioactivity of cargo molecules. To measure the uptake of the Fe-MIL-100 in the fungi mycelia, $5\mathrm{mm}$ diameter mycelial discs of tomato late blight (P. infestans) were inoculated into PDA plates treated with FITC-labelled Fe-MIL-100 (FITC-FeMIL-100) aqueous suspension. After a 5 d culture, the mycelia at the edge of the colony were cautiously excised for visualization observation using CLSM. For a better comparison, FITC-Fe-MIL100 and mycelia without treatment were also observed. As shown in Fig. 11A, FITC-Fe-MIL-100 exhibits an evident fluorescent signal. In Fig. 11C, the mycelia treated with FITC-Fe-MIL-100 reveal a weak fluorescent signal under CLSM; however, no fluorescent signal is observed in the untreated mycelia (Fig. 11B). The foregoing indicates that the Fe-MOFs could function as carriers to deliver pesticide to different fungi.
## 3.6. Nutritional function of Fe-MIL-100 for wheat growth
In view of the atom economy, it is of interest that the pesticide carriers possess multiple functions, including pesticidal bioactivity or crop nutrient replenishment. In particular, it has been reported that Fe-MOFs have been used as iron fertilizer for enhancing plant growth rates [45,46]. In this work, the nutritional functions of FeMIL-100 for wheat growth were also tested. As shown in Fig. 12, compared to the blank control, the plant heights of the aerial part of wheat have achieved $9.6\%$ and $16.4\%$ enhancements when 50 and $300~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ concentrations of Fe-MIL-100 were applied, respectively. These findings clearly indicated that Fe-MIL-100 as iron micronutrient could promote wheat growth. Moreover, it demonstrates the safety in the use of Fe-MIL-100 as a pesticide carrier for wheat growth.
## 4. Conclusions
Although MOFs have been widely investigated as drug delivery vehicles [28,29], their application as pesticide carriers have just started [33–35]. In this study, we firstly demonstrate the feasibility of using Fe-MOFs as multifunctional platforms for both pesticide delivery and plant nutrient replenishment. Porous Fe-MIL-100 was synthesized by a hydrothermal reaction using $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ as the node and 1,3,5-BTC as the organic ligand. AZOX as a model pesticide was loaded into Fe-MIL-100 with the loading content of $16.24\%$ by physical absorption due to the high specific surface area and pore volume. The two materials, Fe-MIL-100 and AZOX@Fe-MIL-100 were well characterized by SEM, XPS, FTIR, TGA, and $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms. The release of AZOX from AZOX@FeMIL-100 is slower in an acidic medium than in neutral and basic media. Compared with AZOX TC and SC, AZOX@Fe-MIL-100 exhibits a comparable fungicidal bioactivity against two types of pathogenic fungi of F. graminearum and P. infestans. The uptake and translocation of Fe-MIL-100 in the fungi mycelia are confirmed by the visual observation of FITC-labelled Fe-MIL-100 with CLSM, demonstrating that Fe-MIL-100 can be utilized as vehicles to deliver pesticide molecules into the mycelia. The nutritional function of Fe-MIL-100 as an iron micronutrient for wheat growth is demonstrated by the $16.4\%$ increase in wheat height. In the future, the concept may be extended to other scalable and cost-effective MOFs with a nutritional function. The preparation and agricultural application of MOFs based on $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ as the node and amino acid as the organic ligand are in progress.
